    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! The lactic acid bacteria and yeast in sourdough fermentation coexist without necessarily fighting each other. Each group of microorganisms has different metabolic needs and produces different by-products, creating a complex environment. Wild yeasts primarily produce carbon dioxide and alcohol during fermentation, contributing to the bread's rise and alcoholic content. Lactobacilli produce lactic acid and other metabolites, developing the unique sour taste.\n\nLactobacilli in the starter help moderate the growth of wild yeast by producing antimicrobial substances that can inhibit unwanted bacterial growth. The yeast benefits from the lactobacilli by using them to create an acidic environment, which some wild yeasts favor. By relying on different sugars and metabolic pathways and producing various waste products, these microorganisms promote a balanced, synergistic fermentation process, helping each other coexist without outcompeting one another.",
